---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ArmyBodyFatCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc/ArmyBodyFatCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="army-body-fat-calculator">
  <ArmyBodyFatCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the Army body fat calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The Army body fat calculator uses the one-site tape test from Army Directive 2023-11, which replaced the
      neck and waist method in 2023. It needs only weight in pounds and the abdomen at the navel in inches. The
      calculator converts kilograms and centimetres for you.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Men: <code>BF% = −26.97 − 0.12 × weight + 1.99 × abdomen</code></li>
      <li>Women: <code>BF% = −9.15 − 0.015 × weight + 1.27 × abdomen</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The older method, kept here for comparison, uses height, neck and abdomen for men
      (<code>BF% = 86.010 × log10(abdomen − neck) − 70.041 × log10(height) + 36.76</code>) and height, neck,
      waist and hips for women
      (<code>BF% = 163.205 × log10(waist + hips − neck) − 97.684 × log10(height) − 78.387</code>), all in inches.
    </p>
    <p>
      The result is compared with the maximum body fat for your age and sex from AR 600-9, the Army Body
      Composition Program: 20% for men aged 17 to 20, 22% for 21 to 27, 24% for 28 to 39 and 26% for 40 and
      over. For women the limits are 30%, 32%, 34% and 36%.
    </p>
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A man of 25 weighs 80 kg (176.4 lb) and has an abdomen of 86 cm (33.86 in). The one-site formula gives
      −26.97 − 0.12 × 176.4 + 1.99 × 33.86 = 19.2%. His maximum is 22%, so he meets the standard with 2.8
      points to spare. With a height of 178 cm and a neck of 38 cm, the older method gives 17.3%.
    </p>
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Soldiers are first checked against a weight-for-height table, and only those over it take the tape test.
      The official test is done by trained staff with repeated tape readings, so your official result can
      differ from this estimate. Tape methods can differ from a lab scan by a few percentage points.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
